One way to explain this assignment is as follows. Our mission is to minimize damage by making sure that as many high‐threat fields are fully protected as possible. Field_2 (threat 0.09) requires 4 drones while Field_3 (threat 0.06) requires 2 drones for full protection. Although Field_4 also has a threat level of 0.06, it requires 3 drones for full protection, and with only eight drones available we cannot fully guard every field. Prioritizing the highest threats and the “cheaper” fields to fully protect we dedicate four drones to Field_2 (using Drone_1, Drone_4, Drone_5, and Drone_8 which are best placed near Field_2’s area), two drones to Field_3 (Drone_6 and Drone_7, the best candidates among the remaining ones), and the leftover two drones (Drone_2 and Drone_3) are assigned to Field_4. Field_4 thus gets partial (2 out of 3 needed) protection – which is not as effective as full coverage, but it is the best compromise given our limited resources. Field_1 (with a very low threat level, 0.03) is left idle.
