One way to think about the assignment is as follows. Field_2 and Field_4 are under the highest bird threat (0.09), and it is crucial to bring them to full protection. Field_2 needs four drones and is already well served by Drone_1, Drone_4, Drone_5, and Drone_8. Field_4 needs three drones; currently two are coming (the drones already assigned to Field_4, namely Drone_2 and Drone_3). Allocating one additional drone (here, Drone_6) to Field_4 completes its full protection. With 8 drones available, the remaining two assignments must come from the group originally protecting Field_3. Field_3’s threat level is fairly low (0.04) and its full protection requirement is just two drones, but we give up on completely covering low‐threat Field_3 in favour of reducing the risk on Field_1 (threat 0.06) by assigning Drone_7 there. Although a single drone only offers partial protection (which is “not very effective” in forcing the birds away) it is still better than leaving a higher threat field (Field_1) unprotected. 

This arrangement yields full protection on the two highest‐threat fields (Field_2 and Field_4) while directing the “leftover effort” toward mitigating damage from Field_1 (threat 0.06) rather than letting Field_3 (threat 0.04) remain attended.
