One way to think about this assignment is that we want to use our limited fleet of 8 drones to “fully protect” as many high‐threat fields as possible so that the birds immediately flee rather than “scattering” when only partially deterred. Field_3 (threat level 0.19) already has exactly the 2 drones needed for full protection, so we leave Drone_1 and Drone_3 there. Next, among the remaining fields Field_1 (threat level 0.12), Field_2 (threat 0.07) and Field_4 (threat 0.11), our best option is to make Field_1 fully protected because it is relatively higher threat than Field_2 and because our drones’ current positions make Field_1 a more accessible target. Field_1 requires 4 drones for full protection and already has two assigned (Drone_2 and Drone_6). We then take two drones from the group protecting Field_2 (choosing, for example, Drone_4 and Drone_7) and reassign them to Field_1 so that Field_1 gets 4 drones and becomes fully protected. That leaves the remaining two drones in Field_2 (Drone_5 and Drone_8) still protecting that field (though not fully, but a partial assignment still helps more than leaving a field unprotected). Field_4 remains unassigned because it would require 3 drones for full protection and we cannot cover every field completely. This allocation minimizes the overall expected field damage by ensuring the fields with the highest threats (Field_3 and Field_1) are fully protected while the lower‐threat fields (Field_2 at 0.07 and Field_4 at 0.11) receive less attention.

