One strategic approach is as follows. Field_1 has the highest threat (0.14) and is already fully protected with 4 drones, so we keep Drone_1, Drone_2, Drone_3 and Drone_5 there. Field_3 and Field_4 currently are guarded by 2 drones each, but Field_4—a threat level of 0.11—needs 3 drones for full protection, while Field_3’s threat is only 0.09 (and requires 2). Since we have only eight drones total, we choose to “upgrade” protection on the more urgent Field_4 by reassigning one drone from Field_3. In this plan, Drone_8—preferably the one closer to Field_4—is reassigned from Field_3 to Field_4 so that Field_4 becomes fully protected (with Drone_6, Drone_7 and Drone_8), while Field_3 remains partly covered with Drone_4. Field_2 remains unguarded as its threat level (0.09) is lower and we cannot cover every field fully with only eight drones.

This grouping prioritizes full protection where it matters most to minimize potential damage.
